+//  Decoded contigs shared by the search threads.  A contig is decoded once (reverse-complemented
+//  for the A side of the minus strand) and then only read, so threads working on slabs of the same
+//  contig pair share it instead of each decoding its own copy.  The aligner may probe a few
+//  positions beyond the end of a contig (a box can start past the end); the byte before the
+//  sequence and the CONTIG_TAIL bytes after it are therefore set to the end-of-sequence code,
+//  so what lies beyond the terminator is the same whatever contigs were processed before (a
+//  per-thread buffer used to keep the tail of the previously loaded, longer contig there).
+//
+//  Only the contigs referenced by running tasks have to stay resident.  A cache that keeps
+//  released contigs up to a fixed size simply adds that size to the peak RSS (6 GiB, filled on
+//  every pair tried, 3.7 GiB above the original FastGA on maize B73 x Mo17), while what it saves
+//  depends on the task order: in the order of the sorted array, with a small cache, maize Mo17
+//  against B73 decodes about 9 TB, since every A contig hits most large B contigs (see
+//  block_order, which brings this to some hundred GB).  Released contigs are therefore kept only
+//  in memory the process has already used at its peak (see cc_allow), and at least
+//  CONTIG_CACHE_BYTES of them, enough for the small contigs that neighbouring contig pairs share.
